# Local biomedical image review workspace — revised proposal

**Status:** Native candidate revision for the assigned role. This is a research plan, not a built or qualified application. It does not diagnose patients.

**Evidence limitation:** `inputs/seed/proposal.md` and `inputs/source_context/index.json` were readable. The original four catalogs and the attached critic artifacts could not be read at the attempted `inputs/seed/{sources,witnesses,leads,dependencies}.json` and `inputs/prior/SEED-DEV-A-DEFERRED-R001-seed-critique-a001/...` paths. The current catalogs below are reconstructed from the proposal and available source index/captures; they are not byte-preserving replacements for inaccessible catalogs. I therefore cannot claim that every critic finding was reviewed. This revision corrects source-backed issues found independently and records unresolved points explicitly.

## Recommendation

Build a small, local desktop application around a project directory, a read-only bounded image-reader service, an explicit per-image coordinate frame, and a command-based annotation model. Start with TIFF/BigTIFF, OME-TIFF, ImageJ hyperstack TIFF, and local OME-Zarr 0.4/0.5, subject to a fixture-based support matrix. Use tifffile as the TIFF adapter, a Zarr adapter for NGFF, and borrow napari’s layer and display-state concepts; do not claim that those libraries together constitute a tested application. Keep QuPath as a useful project/export precedent and a plausible alternative if whole-slide formats become central.

The plan remains incremental. The first milestone is safe import, metadata review, calibrated navigation, point/polygon annotation, save/reopen, and CSV/GeoJSON export with a manifest. Add masks, derived-image export, richer style management, and volume-wide tools after coordinate round-trip and crash-recovery invariants pass. Keyboard navigation, text-readable status, undo/redo, and display/data separation are part of the first usable release.

## Evidence and interpretation

**External facts.** OME-NGFF 0.5 defines 2–5 axes with named dimensions, axis order matching array order, and multiscale transforms composed in list order. In multiscales it restricts transforms to scale and translation, with translation after scale (S1). This is a useful, constrained interchange vocabulary, not a general affine model. The captured napari image, units, and shapes documentation describes lazy NumPy-like data, contrast limits as visualization state, world units, and nD shape layers (S2–S4). These are separate implementation/documentation lines of evidence for view and annotation concepts, not proof of application persistence or performance.

Tifffile’s captured README says it can read image data as NumPy or Zarr arrays from TIFF strips, tiles, pages, SubIFDs, and pyramidal levels (S5). It also documents important limits and a changing API: its 2026.5.2 notes describe breaking Zarr v3/NGFF updates (S5). Therefore the 3 GB goal is an engineering target for our adapter and actual fixtures, not a property inferred from the README. Pin the reader and its codec/Zarr environment, and test the exact adapter calls before adoption.

QuPath documentation provides useful precedents for project entries and external image URIs (S15), explicit full-resolution pixel-coordinate export semantics (S14), and relocation/recovery history (S13). Those details inform our design, but QuPath-specific persistence and recovery behavior must not be attributed to this application.

**Consequential calibration lesson.** Issue #319 reports a TVIPS `PixelSizeX` change between tifffile 2026.2.20 and 2026.2.24. In the issue comments the maintainer says the change was intended as a conversion from nm to m, then says they will revert it; the 2026-03-03 fix commit comments out the division and links the issue. A preceding test commit adds `test_issue_tvips_pixelsize`, whose docstring says the value is nm and whose assertions preserve the fixture values. The release commit is `503cb4eb74dd28a6acc36a0e4624ba9c2001e021`, v2026.3.3, and its release notes say “Do not convert TVIPS pixel sizes to m (#319)” (S7–S11). This supports the lesson that a reader upgrade can change downstream calibration behavior and that a file/reader-specific fixture is valuable. It does **not** establish a universal manufacturer unit rule, prove all releases between the observed versions behaved identically, or establish that the test asset is locally available to this project. The issue title says 2026.2.26 while its body reports 2026.2.24; retain that discrepancy rather than silently harmonizing it. The history is a maintainer commit sequence, not a pull-request merge chain.

## Data contract and workflow

A project contains a small versioned manifest, source registry, annotation records, operation history, optional derived images, exports, and disposable cache. Import opens sources read-only and never rewrites originals. For each source, keep the original path/URI, file size, whole-file digest when computed, reader/version, parsed metadata, original metadata payload or a lossless retained copy, and the user's explicit correction decisions. If parsing fails or axes/calibration conflict, preserve an unresolved source entry and the metadata; show the conflict in text and permit annotation only after an explicit “uncalibrated” or correction choice.

**Coordinate authority:** store annotation geometry as binary64 coordinates in the source array’s declared axis order, bound to an immutable source identity and frame revision. Define index 0 as the center of the first sample. Record a single explicit pixel/index-to-world mapping and convention, including named axes, array order, units, origin, orientation, and dimensionality. Prefer one homogeneous affine matrix `world = M × [index,1]` in a declared row/column-vector convention; do not simultaneously apply a per-axis scale/translation and an unnamed affine. Provide explicit named-axis adapters for screen x/y, z, time, and channel so display order cannot be mistaken for storage order. Store channel and time/slice scope separately from spatial vertices. Physical coordinates are derived through this frame, not copied as a second authority. A geometric transform or resampling creates a new derived source identity and an operation record; changing window/level, LUT, visible channels, slice, or zoom changes view state only.

NGFF import/export should map only the spec’s supported scale-then-translation subset. An input needing rotation/shear, custom axes, or ambiguous calibration is preserved and marked unsupported/ambiguous for calibrated editing until the user selects an explicit conversion path. Do not emit an NGFF-conforming transform that drops such information.

Annotations have stable IDs, kind (point, polygon, mask), scope (plane with z/t, volume with optional t, or acquisition), style ID, frame revision, and source digest. Undo/redo applies to annotation and correction commands. Reopening verifies source identity before displaying annotations as attached. If the source differs, keep it unresolved or require an explicit relink that marks the annotation set suspect. On relocation, try project-relative paths and user-selected candidates; matching a content digest permits relinking, while path/name/size alone does not prove identity.

Exports include CSV or GeoJSON for vectors, a raster overlay where appropriate, and a small derived image. Each export has a manifest naming source identity, coordinate convention and units, axis order, frame revision, transforms/resampling, and any display mapping baked into rendered pixels. GeoJSON must carry an explicit non-geographic coordinate note/CRS policy. Report what the format omits (for example, a rendered PNG cannot retain original intensity samples or editable vector semantics). Re-import a fixture and compare coordinates within a specified serialization tolerance.

## Architecture, durability, and support boundary

Use Python/Qt for the desktop shell only after a separately pinned GUI spike; use workers for decoding, hashing, and export. The reader API accepts a source, level, plane/time/channel selection, and bounded region. An LRU tile/chunk cache has a configurable memory ceiling, viewport-only prefetch, progress, and cancellation. No import step creates a full decoded duplicate. Test the 3 GB target against the actual instrument corpus with peak RSS, tile latency, cancellation latency, and partial-read correctness. Optional GPU rendering is not a requirement for v1.

Use same-directory temporary writes and a generation-based commit: write and flush new data, retain the previous complete generation, then atomically publish a small manifest naming the committed generation. On reopen, select the last valid generation and report recovery. A per-file replace alone does not make a multi-file project transaction atomic. The POSIX rename source documents same-filesystem replacement and NFS caveats (S16); it does not establish identical guarantees on every desktop OS or shared-storage provider. Verify file and directory flush behavior and failure recovery on the actual supported platforms and shared storage. Export to a temporary sibling and publish only on success; preserve the prior completed export. Do not state that a partial file is recoverable unless recovery tests prove it.

For v1, accept locally readable TIFF-family images only when the selected reader/codec combination passes shape, dtype, metadata, and bounded-region fixtures; accept OME-Zarr v0.4 (Zarr v2) and v0.5 (Zarr v3) only through separately tested adapters. Defer vendor slide stitching, OJPEG, ambiguous nonstandard axes, unsupported units, and inputs that require silent guessing. Unsupported input remains registered with diagnostic details and can be retried or exported as a metadata report; it must not invalidate the rest of the project. Never imply “TIFF support” means every TIFF encoding is supported.

Default to references for large sources and offer explicit project copies for smaller sources; communicate portability consequences per source. Hashing 3 GB at import has a cost, so show progress and allow cancellation while keeping the source unresolved until identity is established. Any faster reopen fingerprint is a cache hint, not a substitute for content identity when exporting or relinking. Shared bundles are single-writer in v1; concurrent edits and cloud sync are out of scope.

## Choices, opportunity, alternative, and validation

**Product choices:** native project directory; immutable source images; binary64 index coordinates plus one declared frame transform; local-only single-writer projects; references by default and explicit copies for portable bundles; no universal-format promise. These are reversible choices, not external facts.

**Opportunity:** make derived images and interoperable labels available as OME-Zarr when the selected subset fits NGFF 0.5. Its axes and transform constraints give downstream tools a clear contract (S1); export only after round-trip tests prove no transform or unit information was lost. **Alternative:** adopt napari as a plugin/platform if implementing the viewer is the schedule risk. Its documented lazy-layer, units, and shape mechanisms are relevant (S2–S4), but persistence, project recovery, and application-level performance remain our responsibilities. QuPath remains an alternative if whole-slide support outweighs volume/time-series workflow fit (S13–S15).

A candidate-authored arithmetic check (W4) distinguishes transform order and explicit conversion. For index `[2,3]`, scale `[0.5,2]`, translation `[10,-4]`, scale then translation gives `[11,2]`; translation then scale gives `[6,-2]`. A separate multiplication check gives approximately `1.4208036661148072e-9` when the observed numeric value `1.4208036661148071` is explicitly multiplied by `1e-9`; this arithmetic does not determine the source unit. W4 is isolated arithmetic only, not reader or application evidence.

**Proposed, UNEXECUTED validation:** (1) fixtures for every accepted instrument/codec, including axes, unit, dtype, and source metadata; (2) import/read 3 GB data under 16 GB RAM with peak-RSS and p95 tile/cancel targets; (3) coordinate round-trip across slices, volumes, axes reorder, and export/import; (4) kill during source registration, save, and export on each supported filesystem, then reopen the last complete generation; (5) relocation with correct, changed, and missing source; (6) keyboard-only operation and legible error/metadata review with researchers. Initial target proposals are p95 visible-tile latency below 100 ms, cancellation below 250 ms, and peak process RSS below 4 GB; these are acceptance targets to validate, not measured results. No full application was built.

